## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to architect edge-aware NLP systems under real-world constraints including privacy, multilingual code-switching, per-tenant customization, and safe deployment practices. It tests architectural thinking beyond pure algorithm design.

## Key Concepts
- Edge/on-device inference with optional cloud offload for edge cases
- Code-switching detection and multilingual robustness
- Per-tenant adapters with domain-specific vocabulary mapping
- Drift detection and canary rollout strategies
- Privacy-by-design principles and secure rollback mechanisms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton architecture (not production-ready)
class MultilingualSentimentModule {
  constructor(config) {
    this.encoder = new OnDeviceEncoder(config.model);
    this.codeSwitchDetector = new CodeSwitchDetector();
    this.classifier = new SentimentIntentClassifier();
    this.adapters = new PerTenantAdapterRegistry();
    this.driftMonitor = new DriftDetector();
  }

  async process(text, tenantId, context) {
    const langPair = await this.codeSwitchDetector.detect(text);
    const encoded = await this.encoder.encode(text, langPair);
    const adapted = await this.adapters.apply(tenantId, encoded);
    const result = await this.classifier.predict(adapted);
    this.driftMonitor.record(tenantId, result);
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Architecture Overview

**On-Device Layer**: Compact transformer (e.g., distilled BERT variant or MobileNet-style architecture) runs entirely on the user's device. This ensures no raw text transmission.

**Code-Switching Detector**: Lightweight model that identifies EN/ES transitions and language boundaries, routing each segment to the appropriate model variant.

**Per-Tenant Adapters**: Small adapter layers (e.g., LoRA-style) trained on tenant-specific data, injected between the encoder and classifier.

**Cloud Offload**: When encountering rare languages or ambiguous sentiment, the system can dispatch minimal embeddings (not raw text) to cloud models, maintaining privacy.

**Drift Detection**: Statistical monitoring of prediction distributions per tenant; triggers alerts when KL-divergence exceeds thresholds.

**Canary Rollout**: Gradual traffic shifting (1% → 5% → 25% → 100%) with automated rollback if error rates spike or drift metrics exceed limits.

## Evaluation Plan

1. **A/B Testing**: Compare on-device vs. cloud predictions on validation sets per tenant
2. **Live Feedback Loops**: Surface prediction confidence; collect explicit user corrections
3. **Synthetic Drift Probes**: Inject artificially drifted test data to validate rollback triggers
4. **Privacy Audit**: Verify no PII leaves the device boundary via differential privacy testing
